---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeRadixText from '@/components/tools/dev/DevEncodeRadixText.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="decimal-to-text">
  <DevEncodeRadixText client:load radix="decimal" direction="decode" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Decimal character codes</h2>
    <p>
      Some systems give you a list of plain numbers instead of hex: <code>72 101 108 108 111</code>. Each
      is one byte, 0–255. This page converts decimal to text and text to decimal.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Range</th><th>Contents</th><th>Anchors worth remembering</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>0–31</td><td>Control codes</td><td>9 tab, 10 line feed, 13 carriage return</td></tr>
          <tr><td>32</td><td>Space</td><td>The first printable code</td></tr>
          <tr><td>48–57</td><td>Digits</td><td>48 is <code>0</code>, so 53 is <code>5</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>65–90</td><td>A–Z</td><td>65 is <code>A</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>97–122</td><td>a–z</td><td>97 is <code>a</code>, exactly 32 above <code>A</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>128–255</td><td>Extended</td><td>The meaning depends on the encoding</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Separators are not optional</h2>
    <p>
      Unlike hex, decimal byte values have no fixed width: <code>7</code>, <code>72</code> and
      <code>255</code> have one, two and three digits. So <code>72101108</code> is ambiguous. Use spaces,
      commas, semicolons or newlines; all are accepted. The one exception is input where each value is
      padded to three digits, which the parser can split reliably.
    </p>

    <h2>Codes above 255</h2>
    <p>
      Numbers such as 8364 or 128512 are not bytes; they are Unicode codepoints. 8364 is U+20AC, the euro
      sign; 128512 is U+1F600, the grinning face. This page works on bytes, so it rejects those values and
      does not truncate them. Convert codepoints to UTF-8 bytes first, or use the hex tools, which show the
      multi-byte sequences.
    </p>

    <h2>Where these lists come from</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Spreadsheets: a column made with <code>CODE()</code> or <code>UNICODE()</code>.</li>
      <li>Databases: <code>ASCII()</code> output while you look for an encoding problem.</li>
      <li>Teaching material: introductory exercises almost always use decimal.</li>
      <li>Microcontroller serial output: <code>Serial.println(byte)</code> prints decimal by default, so Arduino logs look like this.</li>
      <li>Puzzles and CTFs: a comma-separated list of numbers is the most common first-stage encoding.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why 65 for A, and why the 32 gap</h2>
    <p>
      ASCII was designed to make operations cheap in hardware. Capital <code>A</code> is 1000001 in binary
      and lowercase <code>a</code> is 1100001: one bit apart. Case conversion is one bitwise operation, and
      32 is the decimal value of that bit. For the same reason the digits are at 48–57, so the numeric value
      of a digit character is its code minus 48.
    </p>

    <h2>Going the other way</h2>
    <p>
      Use the swap button to turn text into a list of decimal codes. In UTF-8 mode, one accented character
      gives two numbers and an emoji gives four. This explains many &ldquo;why is my string longer than I
      typed&rdquo; bugs.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
